# Getting Started

niceusb emulates USB devices (keyboard, mouse, gamepad) on an RP2350, driven by
a script you write here. The emulated device plugs into this PC, and the
**HID Report Inspector** shows what the host actually received — closing the loop.

## Quick start

1. Wire the Raspberry Pi Debug Probe to the Pico 2 (SWD) and plug both this PC
   and the Pico's native USB port in.
2. Click **Attach** (Controls window). The console shows `attached (RTT 9090)`
   and a `heartbeat` every second.
3. Type a script in the **Script Editor**, e.g.:

```
type "hello world"
```

4. Click **Run**. The emulated keyboard types it; the **HID Report Inspector**
   shows the decoded characters the host received.
5. Click **Detach** to release the probe (e.g. before running a command-line
   tool that needs it).

## The panes

- **Controls** — Attach / Run / Detach and probe status.
- **Script Editor** — your WUSB script.
- **HID Report Inspector** — live, decoded view of what the host received;
  filter by Keyboard / Mouse / Gamepad, or Clear.
- **Console** — device logs, `OK: script done`, and `ERR line N: ...` messages.
- **Documentation** — this window.

All panes are dockable: drag a tab to rearrange, split, or tear off. The layout
is remembered between runs.

See **WUSB Reference** for the full scripting language. On the Adafruit Fruit Jam,
see **Standalone Mode (SD Card)** to run scripts from a card with no PC attached.
# WUSB Reference

Scripts are line-oriented: one action per line. `#` starts a comment; blank lines
are ignored. The script runs on the device; errors come back as
`ERR line N: <message>` and execution stops at the first error.

A script starts in **keyboard** mode. Switch device with `device`. Verbs only work
in their device's mode (e.g. `move` needs `device mouse`).

## Device

```
device keyboard | mouse | gamepad   # re-enumerate as that single device
connect                             # (re)attach to the USB bus
disconnect                          # drop off the bus
```

Switching device re-enumerates on the host (keyboard = PID 0x4001,
mouse = 0x4002, gamepad = 0x4003).

## Keyboard

```
key tap A            # press and release
key down LSHIFT      # hold
key up LSHIFT
type "Hello!"        # types the string (handles shift/symbols)
delay 100ms          # also: 2s, 500us
repeat 5 { key tap A }   # bounded loop (single level; inline or multi-line)
```

Named keys: `A`–`Z`, `0`–`9`, `ENTER`, `SPACE`, `TAB`, `ESC`, `BACKSPACE`,
`LSHIFT`, `RSHIFT`, `LCTRL`, `LALT`, `LGUI`.

`type` escapes: `\n` (Enter), `\t` (Tab), `\"`, `\\`.

```
repeat 3 {
  type "line"
  key tap ENTER
}
```

## Mouse

```
move 50 -20          # relative dx dy
click left           # left | right | middle
button down right    # hold / release a button
button up right
scroll 3             # wheel (signed)
```

## Gamepad

```
axis x 100           # x|y|z|rz|rx|ry, value -127..127 (clamped)
pad button 0 down    # button index 0..31
pad button 0 up
hat up               # center|up|upright|right|downright|down|downleft|left|upleft
```

## Example: switch devices in one script

```
device keyboard
type "login\n"
delay 200ms
device mouse
move 100 0
click left
device gamepad
axis x 127
pad button 0 down
```
# App Guide

## Connection model

The app talks to the RP2350 two ways:

- **Control channel (SWD):** the app launches OpenOCD and opens a SEGGER RTT
  channel over the debug probe to push your script text and read logs. **Attach**
  starts this; **Detach** stops it and frees the probe.
- **Loopback (native USB):** the emulated device plugs into this PC; the host's
  input stack receives its reports, which the inspector reads via Win32 Raw Input.

Only one program can own the debug probe at a time. If **Attach** fails with
`RTT connect failed`, make sure no other OpenOCD / `rtt.ps1` / E2E script is
running, then try again.

## Workflow

1. **Attach** → wait for `attached (RTT 9090)` and heartbeats.
2. Write a script, **Run** it.
3. Watch the **HID Report Inspector** for what the host received, and the
   **Console** for `OK: script done` or `ERR line N: ...`.
4. **Detach** when you need the probe elsewhere.

## Inspector

- Lines are tagged by type: `KBD`, `MOUSE`, `PAD`.
- Toggle **Keyboard / Mouse / Gamepad** to filter; **Clear** wipes the view.
- Keyboard shows decoded characters; mouse shows dx/dy/buttons; gamepad shows
  axes/hat/buttons.

## Notes

- In interactive mode the app does not steal focus, so emulated keystrokes also
  land in whatever window is focused — click into the editor or another window as
  needed. (Automated self-tests grab focus so they don't leak.)
- Switching device type re-enumerates; the host takes ~1s to be ready, which the
  firmware waits out before sending reports.

## Command-line self-tests

Built-in headless end-to-end checks (used by the `tools/e2e_*.ps1` harnesses):

```
niceusb.exe --selftest-wusb  <file>   # keyboard: type "m2ok"
niceusb.exe --selftest-mouse <file>   # mouse: move + click
niceusb.exe --selftest-pad   <file>   # gamepad: axis + button
```
# How HID USB Devices Work

HID (Human Interface Device) is the USB class for devices a person operates:
keyboards, mice, gamepads, dials, touchpads, and a huge range of vendor gadgets.
Its defining trait is that the device is self-describing: it ships a machine-readable
description of its own data, so the operating system needs no per-device driver. Plug
in any conformant HID device and it works.

This page explains the moving parts and where each one shows up in niceusb. Doc 05
is the deep reference for the report descriptor itself.

## Why HID is self-describing

A USB host that wanted to support a new mouse used to need a new driver. HID removes
that: the device hands the host a report descriptor, a compact grammar that says
"my data comes in packets shaped like this, and this field means X". The host's
generic HID driver reads that grammar once at plug-in and then knows how to interpret
every packet. The device defines the contract; the host obeys it.

That is exactly why niceusb is useful: by editing the descriptors the device presents,
you control that contract and can test how a host reacts to it.

## The descriptor stack

When a HID device is enumerated, the host reads a nested set of descriptors. Each
layer narrows from "what is this device" down to "how do its data packets look":

```
Device descriptor        identity: idVendor, idProduct, bcdDevice, class
  Configuration descriptor   power, number of interfaces
    Interface descriptor       bInterfaceClass = 0x03 (HID)
      HID class descriptor       bcdHID, and the length of the report descriptor
      Endpoint descriptor        Interrupt IN  (device -> host reports)
      Endpoint descriptor        Interrupt OUT (host -> device, optional)
    String descriptors         human-readable Manufacturer / Product / Serial
```

- The device descriptor is the identity card: who made it and what it is.
- The configuration groups one or more interfaces. A HID interface has class 0x03.
- The HID class descriptor sits inside the interface and, crucially, tells the host
  the type and length of the report descriptor to fetch next.
- Endpoints are the data pipes. HID uses an interrupt IN endpoint for the device to
  send reports to the host, and optionally an interrupt OUT endpoint for the reverse.
- String descriptors hold the UTF-16 text shown in Device Manager and the like.

The report descriptor is fetched separately (it is not part of the configuration
descriptor) using the length advertised by the HID class descriptor.

## Reports: the actual data

Once parsed, the device and host exchange reports: small fixed-shape packets. There
are three kinds:

- Input report: device -> host. A key press, a mouse delta, a stick position.
- Output report: host -> device. Keyboard LEDs, rumble, indicator lights.
- Feature report: configuration/state, exchanged either direction on demand (not
  part of the normal input stream).

The report descriptor is the schema for these bytes. It declares, field by field,
how many bits each value uses, what range it spans, and which usage (semantic
meaning, e.g. "X axis" or "Button 1") it carries. Without the descriptor, an input
report is just anonymous bytes; with it, the host knows byte 0 bit 0 is the left
mouse button.

## Enumeration and parsing, step by step

```
1. Host reads the device descriptor              -> sees class / identity
2. Host reads the configuration descriptor       -> finds the HID interface + endpoints
3. Host reads the HID class descriptor           -> learns report-descriptor length
4. Host fetches the report descriptor            -> the field-by-field schema
5. Host builds a parser from that schema
6. Device sends input reports on the interrupt IN endpoint; the host decodes each
   one against the schema and delivers events to applications
```

Steps 1-4 happen once at plug-in. Step 6 repeats for the life of the connection.

## Boot vs report protocol

Some HID devices (keyboards and mice) also support a fixed, descriptor-free format
called the boot protocol, so a BIOS can use them before any HID parser exists. The
richer, descriptor-driven format is the report protocol, which is what the OS uses
normally. Doc 05 covers the boot report layouts.

## How this maps to niceusb

Every layer above is something you can inspect or author here:

- Descriptor Editor -> Device / Configuration / Strings tabs: edit the device,
  configuration (interfaces/endpoints/HID class descriptor), and string descriptors
  directly, as forms kept in sync with the raw bytes.
- Descriptor Editor -> HID Report tab: build or edit the report descriptor as a
  decoded tree and as raw hex, with live validation. This is the schema from step 4.
- Push to device: send the edited descriptor set to the Pico over RTT so it
  re-enumerates presenting exactly those descriptors.
- HID Report Inspector: watch the input reports the host actually receives, decoded
  against the device's report descriptor (step 6).
- USB Devices (browse + clone): read the descriptors of any connected USB device and
  clone them into the editor. Note: Windows does not expose a device's raw report
  descriptor, only its parsed form, so the cloned HID report descriptor is
  reconstructed approximately from that parsed data (it is flagged as such). The
  device/configuration/string descriptors are copied faithfully.
- WUSB device modes: `device keyboard | mouse | gamepad` make the Pico present a
  ready-made HID device of that type, each with its own report descriptor.

To see the whole loop: clone or author a descriptor set, push it, then open the HID
Report Inspector and watch the reports decode against the schema you defined.
# HID Report Descriptors (Reference)

The report descriptor is the grammar a HID device uses to describe its reports. It
is a flat byte stream of items that a parser walks left to right, accumulating
state, until main items emit fields into a report. This is the deep reference; doc 04
is the conceptual overview. The Descriptor Editor's HID Report tab decodes and
re-encodes exactly this structure.

## The item model

A descriptor is a sequence of items. Almost all are short items: a one-byte prefix
followed by 0, 1, 2, or 4 data bytes.

```
prefix byte:  bbbb tt ss
  ss  bits[1:0]  data size:  0 -> 0 bytes, 1 -> 1, 2 -> 2, 3 -> 4
  tt  bits[3:2]  item type:  0 Main, 1 Global, 2 Local, 3 Reserved
  bbbb bits[7:4] item tag (meaning within that type)
```

So `75 08` is prefix 0x75 (tag 0111, type Global, size 1) + data 0x08 = "Report
Size = 8". A long item (prefix 0xFE) carries a length byte, a tag byte, then up to
255 data bytes; it is rare and niceusb keeps any it cannot model as a raw item.

Items fall into three families: Global, Local, and Main.

## Global items (persistent state)

Global items set parser state that persists until changed. They describe the fields
that following main items will emit.

```
04  Usage Page          which usage namespace following Usages live in
14  Logical Minimum     min value a field reports (as stored)
24  Logical Maximum     max value a field reports
34  Physical Minimum    real-world value mapped to Logical Minimum
44  Physical Maximum    real-world value mapped to Logical Maximum
54  Unit Exponent       power-of-ten exponent for the unit
64  Unit                physical unit encoding (length, time, etc.)
74  Report Size         bits per field
84  Report ID           start an ID'd report (see Report IDs below)
94  Report Count        number of fields of this size
A4  Push                save the whole global state onto a stack
B4  Pop                 restore the last pushed global state
```

Report Size x Report Count = the number of bits the next main item contributes.

## Local items (reset after each main item)

Local items qualify the very next main item and are cleared once it is emitted.

```
08  Usage               semantic meaning of a field (e.g. X, Button)
18  Usage Minimum       start of a usage range
28  Usage Maximum       end of a usage range
38  Designator Index    index into physical designators
48  Designator Minimum
58  Designator Maximum
78  String Index        index of an associated string descriptor
88  String Minimum
98  String Maximum
A8  Delimiter           open/close a set of alternate usages
```

Because locals reset, you re-state Usages before every main item. Globals do not
reset, which is why a descriptor sets Usage Page or Report Size once and reuses it.

## Main items (emit fields)

Main items consume the accumulated global + local state and emit fields, or open and
close collections.

```
80  Input               a field reported device -> host
90  Output              a field reported host -> device
B0  Feature             a field exchanged on demand
A0  Collection          open a grouping (data byte = type, below)
C0  End Collection      close the current grouping
```

The data byte of Input/Output/Feature is a bit field of attributes:

```
bit0  Data        / Constant     constant = padding / read-only
bit1  Array       / Variable     variable = one field per usage; array = an index
bit2  Absolute    / Relative     relative = delta since last report (e.g. mouse)
bit3  No Wrap     / Wrap
bit4  Linear      / Nonlinear
bit5  Preferred   / No Preferred
bit6  No Null     / Null state
bit7  Nonvolatile / Volatile     (Output/Feature only)
bit8  Bit field   / Buffered bytes
```

The Array vs Variable distinction matters most. A Variable field maps each report
bit/value to one usage (16 buttons = 16 one-bit usages). An Array field reports an
index that selects one of N usages (a keyboard's 6-key rollover: six byte slots,
each holding the usage of a pressed key). niceusb's clone reconstruction emits a
usage range with report count > 1 as an Array, not a Variable.

## Collections

Collections group fields into a tree. `A0` opens, `C0` closes; the open's data byte
is the type:

```
00  Physical     a set of data for one physical point (e.g. a mouse pointer)
01  Application   top-level grouping the OS routes to one consumer (Mouse, Keyboard)
02  Logical       a set of items that belong together as one data structure
03  Report        groups items sharing a Report ID
04  Named Array
05  Usage Switch
06  Usage Modifier
```

Every descriptor begins with an Application collection naming the device's top-level
usage (e.g. Generic Desktop / Mouse).

## Usages and usage pages

A usage is the semantic tag on a field. It is a 32-bit value: the high 16 bits are
the usage page (the namespace) and the low 16 bits are the usage ID within it.

```
usage = (page << 16) | id
```

Common pages:

```
01  Generic Desktop   X, Y, Z, Wheel, Pointer, Mouse, Keyboard, Joystick, Game Pad
02  Simulation
07  Keyboard / Keypad   one ID per key
08  LEDs                Num Lock, Caps Lock, ...
09  Button              Button 1, Button 2, ...
0C  Consumer            volume, media transport, app launch
```

The Descriptor Editor resolves page+id to names where it can.

## Report IDs

By default an interface has one input report with no ID, and reports are just the
field bytes. If the descriptor includes a Report ID global item, the device supports
multiple distinct reports on the same interface, and every report is prefixed with
its one-byte ID:

```
no Report ID:   [ field bytes ... ]
with Report ID: [ id ][ field bytes for that id ... ]
```

A keyboard with a separate LED output report and a consumer-control report is a
typical multi-report device. (Note: Windows Raw Input prepends a report-ID byte even
when the device uses no report IDs, so a raw capture's first byte may be a leading 0.)

## Boot vs report protocol

Keyboards and mice may declare a boot interface (bInterfaceSubClass = 1, with a boot
protocol) so a BIOS can use them before any HID parser runs. Boot reports have fixed
layouts independent of the report descriptor:

```
Boot keyboard report (8 bytes):
  byte 0     modifier bitmap (Ctrl/Shift/Alt/GUI, left & right)
  byte 1     reserved
  bytes 2-7  up to 6 keycodes (an array; 0 = no key)

Boot mouse report (3 bytes):
  byte 0     button bitmap
  byte 1     X delta (signed)
  byte 2     Y delta (signed)
```

The OS switches to report protocol after enumeration and then uses the descriptor's
own layout. A custom HID interface (subclass 0) has no boot format and must be
parsed; declaring boot subclass with no boot support is a common cause of a device
failing to start.

## Worked example: a mouse report descriptor

This is the standard boot-style mouse descriptor (the same bytes niceusb's Mouse
template loads). Indentation shows collection nesting:

```
05 01        Usage Page (Generic Desktop)
09 02        Usage (Mouse)
A1 01        Collection (Application)
  09 01        Usage (Pointer)
  A1 00        Collection (Physical)
    05 09        Usage Page (Button)
    19 01        Usage Minimum (Button 1)
    29 03        Usage Maximum (Button 3)
    15 00        Logical Minimum (0)
    25 01        Logical Maximum (1)
    95 03        Report Count (3)
    75 01        Report Size (1)
    81 02        Input (Data, Variable, Absolute)   -> 3 button bits
    95 01        Report Count (1)
    75 05        Report Size (5)
    81 03        Input (Constant, Variable, Absolute) -> 5 padding bits
    05 01        Usage Page (Generic Desktop)
    09 30        Usage (X)
    09 31        Usage (Y)
    15 81        Logical Minimum (-127)
    25 7F        Logical Maximum (127)
    75 08        Report Size (8)
    95 02        Report Count (2)
    81 06        Input (Data, Variable, Relative)   -> X, Y deltas
  C0           End Collection (Physical)
C0           End Collection (Application)
```

That descriptor defines a 3-byte input report:

```
byte 0   bits 0-2 = buttons 1/2/3, bits 3-7 = padding
byte 1   X delta, signed -127..127
byte 2   Y delta, signed -127..127
```

Paste these bytes into the Descriptor Editor's HID Report tab to see them decode into
exactly this tree, then push and watch the 3-byte reports arrive in the HID Report
Inspector.

## How niceusb reconstructs (and where it is approximate)

When you clone a real HID device, Windows only exposes the parsed capabilities
(usages, report sizes/counts, logical ranges), never the original report-descriptor
bytes. niceusb rebuilds a descriptor from those capabilities: an Application
collection, button caps as 1-bit Variable fields (with padding to byte-align),
multi-usage arrays as Array fields, and value caps as sized Variable fields. It
cannot recover nested Physical collections, exact item ordering, or vendor padding,
so the result is functionally close but not byte-identical. The clone preview and the
HID Report tab flag it as approximate.
# Standalone Mode (SD Card)

On the **Adafruit Fruit Jam** (RP2350B), niceusb can run WUSB scripts straight
from a microSD card with **no PC and no debug probe attached** — power it up and
it acts as the emulated device on its own. Scripts are parsed and run on the
RP2350 itself, so the card is the only thing it needs.

Note: standalone mode is Fruit-Jam-only — the Pico 2 has no SD slot. Everything in
the other help topics still applies when a host is attached.

## Quick start

1. Format a microSD card as **FAT32**.
2. Put a script named **`autorun.wusb`** in the card's root, e.g.:

```
device keyboard
connect
type "hello from the SD card"
```

3. Insert the card and power the Fruit Jam from any USB port (no probe needed).
4. The script runs automatically and the emulated keyboard types into whatever
   has focus on the host.

## When the script runs

`/autorun.wusb` runs on any of these:

- **At boot**, if a card is already inserted.
- **On card insert** (if the board booted without one).
- **On a button press** — see below.

## The run button

Press **Button 2** on the Fruit Jam to re-run `/autorun.wusb` on demand. The
**second NeoPixel double-blinks orange** to acknowledge the press, then the
status NeoPixel shows the result.

## Status NeoPixel

The first onboard NeoPixel reports what the device is doing:

- **Dim blue** — booted and idle.
- **Blue** — a script is running.
- **Green** — the last script finished cleanly.
- **Red** — the last script hit an error (see the log).
- **Off** — no card, or no `autorun.wusb` on the card.

## The log file

Every run appends one line to **`/wusb.log`** on the card, so you can pull the
card and see what happened with no host attached:

```
OK: autorun.wusb
ERR line 3: unknown verb (autorun.wusb)
```

## A library of scripts

You can keep other `*.wusb` files on the card alongside `autorun.wusb`. They sit
idle in standalone use, but when a debug probe is attached you can list and run
them by name over the RTT link (`@list`, `@run <name>`) and push the current
editor script to the card (`@put <name>`). Listings are capped at 16 files and
warn if there are more.

## Good to know

- **Keep `autorun.wusb` short.** It runs before the heartbeat loop, so a long or
  device-reconfiguring script delays startup. For typing, prefer a brief
  `device keyboard / connect / type "..."`.
- **Card removal isn't detected** on this board (there's no usable card-detect
  line). Inserting a card into a board that booted empty works, but swapping
  cards (remove, then reinsert) needs a power cycle before autorun picks up the
  new card.
- **File too large.** A script bigger than the on-device buffer (4 KB) is refused
  rather than run truncated.

See **WUSB Reference** for the scripting language and **App Guide** for the
host-attached workflow.
# USB Host & Pass-Through

niceusb can act as a USB **host** — plugging a device-under-test (DUT) into the
board's PIO-USB port and driving it from a script — or as an inline **proxy**
between a real PC and the DUT. Three modes are selectable at runtime:

- **emulate** (default): the RP2350's native USB port presents an emulated HID
  device to the PC, as in all other help topics.
- **host**: PIO-USB port acts as USB host; the DUT plugged in there is enumerated
  and exercised by your script. Native USB is idle.
- **proxy**: both stacks active. The DUT is enumerated over PIO-USB; its
  descriptors are cloned so native USB re-presents the DUT to the PC byte-for-byte.
  The board sits inline between PC and DUT, forwarding traffic both directions,
  capturing everything, and applying optional inject/drop/delay rules.

Port assignment: **native USB = device side** (PC-facing); **PIO-USB = host side**
(DUT-facing). The control channel (SWD/RTT) is unchanged.

Host/proxy mode is Fruit Jam hardware in practice — the Pico 2 has no dedicated
host-power pin.

## Mode selection

```
mode emulate               # back to normal device-emulation
mode host                  # enable host stack, DUT side active
mode proxy                 # MITM bridge: clone DUT to native USB, forward both ways
```

## Host-test verbs

### Enumeration

```
host enumerate             # power port, enumerate DUT, bind drivers
host wait connect 5s       # block until device attaches (or timeout → error)
host reset                 # bus reset + re-enumerate
host info                  # log VID:PID / class / speed / #interfaces to console
```

### Descriptors

```
host get descriptor device        # fetch device descriptor into capture
host get descriptor config        # configuration descriptor
host get descriptor hid           # HID class descriptor
host get descriptor string <i>    # string descriptor at index i
```

### HID class

```
host hid poll 2s                        # read input reports for 2 s, push to capture
host hid get report input|output|feature <id>   # GET_REPORT
host hid set report input|output|feature [01 00 7f]  # SET_REPORT with byte payload
host hid idle <n>                       # SET_IDLE (n = idle rate, 0 = infinite)
```

### Generic control & configuration

```
host control in  0x80 0x06 0x0100 0 64   # bmReqType bRequest wValue wIndex wLen
host control out 0x00 0x09 0x0001 0 []   # OUT with optional byte payload
host set config <n>                       # SET_CONFIGURATION n
```

## Assertions (`expect`)

On-device assertions work in host/proxy mode the same way they work in device-
emulation mode: a failure logs `FAIL line N: …` and stops the script.

```
expect connected                   # DUT is attached
expect vidpid 0x046d 0xc52b        # VID:PID matches
expect class hid                   # device class is HID
expect report input [.. 04 ..]     # last input report matches; '..' is a wildcard byte
expect report-count >= 10          # operators: >=  <=  ==  >  <
expect descriptor hid len 63       # captured HID descriptor has this length
expect stall                       # last transfer was STALLed
expect ack                         # last transfer succeeded
```

## Proxy inject rules

Rules are declared before `proxy run` starts the bridge:

```
mode proxy
on input  report inject [00 00 04 00 00 00 00 00]  # send this extra report to the PC
on input  report drop every 10                      # drop every 10th DUT→PC report
on output report delay 50ms                         # delay PC→DUT OUT reports by 50 ms
on control request stall bRequest 0x09              # force STALL on a chosen request
proxy run 30s                                       # run for up to 30 s (or until stopped)
```

Rules are `match → action` predicates evaluated inside the forwarding tap. Byte-
rewriting is not supported — packets are forwarded, dropped, delayed, injected
alongside, or stalled, but not mutated. If the bridge falls behind (PIO-USB
underrun, ring overflow), it logs a dropped-packet count rather than silently
losing data.

## App UI

- **Mode selector** (toolbar): `Emulate · Host-test · Proxy` — reflects and sets
  the firmware mode.
- **DUT chip**: `DUT: ● 046d:c52b (HID)` next to the Device and Probe chips when
  a device is attached, `○ none` otherwise.
- **Capture inspector**: bidirectional event list replacing the unidirectional HID
  Report Inspector in host/proxy mode. Each row has a direction column (`DUT→` or
  `PC→DUT`) and a color-coded kind tag:

  | Tag | Meaning |
  |-----|---------|
  | `ENUM` | Device connected/enumerated |
  | `IN` | Input report, DUT → PC |
  | `OUT` | Output report, PC → DUT |
  | `CTRL` | Control transfer |
  | `INJECT` | Injected report (from an `inject` rule) |
  | `DROP` | Dropped packet (from a `drop` rule) |
  | `RESULT` | Transfer result (ACK / STALL) |

  Filter by direction or kind; decoded bytes render the same way as in the
  existing HID inspector.
- **Save capture**: exports the event ring (raw + decoded) to a file.
- **DUT descriptor view**: the device/config/HID descriptors the DUT presented
  render in the existing Descriptor Editor (read-only in host/proxy mode).

## Standalone (SD card, Fruit Jam only)

Host and proxy scripts parse and run on the RP2350 itself — no PC or debug probe
required. Place a host or proxy script at `/autorun.wusb` on the SD card and the
Fruit Jam runs it automatically at boot, exactly like any other standalone script.

```
# /autorun.wusb — standalone HID device tester example
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
expect connected
expect class hid
host hid poll 2s
expect report-count >= 1
```

The NeoPixel and log file (`/wusb.log`) report pass/fail the same way they do for
device-emulation scripts. See **Standalone Mode (SD Card)** for the full
standalone workflow.

## Hardware — Fruit Jam pin assignments

PIO-USB host runs on PIO1 at 120 MHz system clock. Pins:

| Signal | GPIO |
|--------|------|
| D+ | 1 |
| D- | 2 |
| 5V host power | 11 |

The RP2350B PIO reaches only a 32-pin window. D+ and D- sit within that window
with no extra configuration needed on Fruit Jam.

## Example scripts

Ready-to-run scripts are in **File → Examples → Host** and **File → Examples →
Proxy** (one device on the host port):

- **Host → Enumerate** — enumerate the device, print VID:PID/class, assert it's HID.
- **Host → Poll Reports** — capture live HID input for 3 s (move/type during the run).
- **Host → Assert Vidpid** — guard a test on the exact device by VID/PID.
- **Host → Descriptor Check** — assert the HID report-descriptor length.
- **Host → Wait For Device** — wait up to 30 s for a hot-plug, then identify it.
- **Host → Report Pattern** — wildcard byte-match the last input report.
- **Proxy → Passthrough** — clone the device to the PC and forward its input through.

Pick one to load it into the Script Editor, then press **Run**. Edit the VID/PID
or descriptor length in the assertion examples to match your device.

## Status & limitations

- **Host-test mode works on hardware (single device):** enumerate, `host info`,
  `host hid poll` with **live HID report capture**, and the `expect` assertions
  all run reliably with one device on the host port.
- **Proxy / pass-through works on hardware (single device):** `mode proxy` clones
  the device-under-test's VID/PID + HID report descriptor onto the native port,
  the PC re-enumerates the clone, and the DUT's input reports are forwarded
  through to the PC (validated end-to-end — the PC cursor moves from the proxied
  device).
- **Multi-device hubs are the current limitation.** With two or more devices on
  the onboard hub, sustained polling triggers a re-enumeration storm (a
  single-core PIO-USB SOF-timing limit). Use one device per host port for now;
  moving the PIO-USB host timing to core 1 is the planned fix for multi-device.
- Not yet implemented (planned): proxy inject rules (`on …`), PC→DUT control
  forwarding, multi-interface/composite cloning, raw-endpoint `host transfer`,
  and standalone-SD autorun of host/proxy scripts.
- The web/Emscripten build is authoring-only; it has no live device connection.

See **WUSB Reference** for all script syntax and **App Guide** for the
host-attached workflow.
# Type a greeting on the host.
device keyboard
connect
type "Hello from FreeWili2 NiceUSB!"
# \n presses Enter, so this types three lines.
device keyboard
connect
type "Line one\nLine two\nLine three\n"
# Shifted punctuation is handled automatically.
device keyboard
connect
type "!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{}|;:',.<>/?"
# Ctrl+C then Ctrl+V (hold the modifier around a tap).
device keyboard
connect
key down LCTRL
key tap C
key up LCTRL
delay 200ms
key down LCTRL
key tap V
key up LCTRL
# Ctrl+A to select everything.
device keyboard
connect
key down LCTRL
key tap A
key up LCTRL
# Ctrl+S to save.
device keyboard
connect
key down LCTRL
key tap S
key up LCTRL
# Alt+Tab to switch windows.
device keyboard
connect
key down LALT
key tap TAB
key up LALT
# Win+R opens the Run dialog, then launch Notepad.
device keyboard
connect
key down LGUI
key tap R
key up LGUI
delay 500ms
type "notepad\n"
# Fill a username/password form: user, Tab, password, Enter.
device keyboard
connect
type "myusername"
key tap TAB
type "s3cr3t-pass"
key tap ENTER
# Count down with one-second pauses.
device keyboard
connect
type "3"
delay 1s
type "2"
delay 1s
type "1"
delay 1s
type "go!\n"
# Repeat a block five times.
device keyboard
connect
repeat 5 {
type "spam "
}
# Jiggle the pointer so the screen never sleeps.
device mouse
connect
repeat 10 {
move 15 0
move -15 0
}
# Move the pointer in a square.
device mouse
connect
move 100 0
move 0 100
move -100 0
move 0 -100
# Press, drag, release with the left button.
device mouse
connect
button down left
move 120 80
button up left
# Two quick left clicks.
device mouse
connect
click left
delay 60ms
click left
# Scroll down, pause, then scroll back up.
device mouse
connect
repeat 5 { scroll -3 }
delay 300ms
repeat 5 { scroll 3 }
# Trace a rough circle with the pointer.
device mouse
connect
move 10 -4
move 8 -8
move 4 -10
move -4 -10
move -8 -8
move -10 -4
move -10 4
move -8 8
move -4 10
move 4 10
move 8 8
move 10 4
# Zig-zag the pointer across the screen.
device mouse
connect
repeat 6 {
move 40 20
move 40 -20
}
# Sweep the left stick around the four cardinal directions.
device gamepad
connect
axis x 100
axis y 0
delay 150ms
axis x 0
axis y 100
delay 150ms
axis x -100
axis y 0
delay 150ms
axis x 0
axis y -100
delay 150ms
axis x 0
axis y 0
# Press a button and work the d-pad (hat).
device gamepad
connect
pad button 0 down
delay 100ms
pad button 0 up
hat up
delay 100ms
hat right
delay 100ms
hat center
# Pull and release the analog triggers (Z / RZ axes).
device gamepad
connect
axis z 127
delay 200ms
axis z 0
delay 200ms
axis rz 127
delay 200ms
axis rz 0
# Rapidly mash button 0.
device gamepad
connect
repeat 8 {
pad button 0 down
delay 40ms
pad button 0 up
delay 40ms
}
# Spin the d-pad (hat) through all eight directions.
device gamepad
connect
hat up
delay 80ms
hat upright
delay 80ms
hat right
delay 80ms
hat downright
delay 80ms
hat down
delay 80ms
hat downleft
delay 80ms
hat left
delay 80ms
hat upleft
delay 80ms
hat center
# Tap gamepad buttons 0 through 7 in turn.
device gamepad
connect
pad button 0 down
pad button 0 up
pad button 1 down
pad button 1 up
pad button 2 down
pad button 2 up
pad button 3 down
pad button 3 up
pad button 4 down
pad button 4 up
pad button 5 down
pad button 5 up
pad button 6 down
pad button 6 up
pad button 7 down
pad button 7 up
# Emit an A4 (440 Hz) tone on the UAC2 mic.
device audio
audio tone 440hz
# Play a C-major scale, then silence (0 Hz).
device audio
audio tone 262hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 294hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 330hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 349hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 392hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 440hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 494hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 523hz
delay 300ms
audio tone 0hz
# Two-tone siren.
device audio
repeat 5 {
audio tone 440hz
delay 250ms
audio tone 660hz
delay 250ms
}
audio tone 0hz
# Three short beeps.
device audio
repeat 3 {
audio tone 880hz
delay 100ms
audio tone 0hz
delay 100ms
}
# Easter egg: stream a hidden looped clip from flash instead of a tone.
# (Open the "niceusb Audio" recording device and have a listen...)
device audio
audio rickroll
# Send a raw 3-byte report on a custom HID device.
device hid
report 0x01 0x7F 0x00
# Load a boot-keyboard report descriptor, then become that HID device.
hid_descriptor {
  05 01 09 06 A1 01 05 07 19 E0 29 E7 15 00 25 01
  75 01 95 08 81 02 95 01 75 08 81 03 05 07 19 00
  29 65 15 00 25 65 75 08 95 06 81 00 C0
}
device hid
# Load a 3-button mouse report descriptor, then become that HID device.
hid_descriptor {
  05 01 09 02 A1 01 09 01 A1 00 05 09 19 01 29 03
  15 00 25 01 95 03 75 01 81 02 95 01 75 05 81 03
  05 01 09 30 09 31 15 81 25 7F 75 08 95 02 81 06 C0 C0
}
device hid
# Override the USB string descriptors (manufacturer / product / serial).
string_descriptor 1 "FreeWili"
string_descriptor 2 "FreeWili2 NiceUSB Device"
string_descriptor 3 "SN-0001"
# Send a raw 18-byte USB device descriptor (VID 0xCAFE / PID 0x4004).
device_descriptor {
  12 01 00 02 00 00 00 40 FE CA 04 40 00 01 01 02 03 01
}
# Act as a USB host: enumerate the device on the host port and assert it's HID.
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
host info
expect connected
expect class hid
# Capture live HID input reports for 3s (move/type the device); expect at least one.
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
host hid poll 3s
expect report-count >= 1
# Guard a device test: assert the exact device by VID/PID (edit for your device).
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
expect connected
expect vidpid 0x1532 0x00b6
# Assert the device's interface-0 HID report-descriptor length (edit the value).
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
host info
expect class hid
expect descriptor hid len 94
# Wait up to 30s for a device to be plugged into the host port, then identify it.
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 30s
expect connected
host info
# Match the last input report against a byte pattern ('..' = wildcard; edit to fit).
mode host
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
host hid poll 3s
expect report-count >= 1
expect report input [.. 04 .. ..]
# Pass-through (MITM): clone the host-port device onto the native USB port so the
# PC sees it, and forward its input through niceusb to the PC for 20s.
mode proxy
host enumerate
host wait connect 5s
proxy run 20s
